{#
	The content part of a signup confirmation (XIV-64), drawn inside the same
	frame every other email from this application uses —
	`@XiviCore/email/base.html.twig`, which supplies the skeleton, the width and
	the footer (§5.13). This file is only what goes in the middle, and it is
	rendered to a string and handed in as `content` by `SignupMailer`.

	Inline styles for the same reason the frame uses them: Gmail and Outlook both
	drop a <style> block often enough that relying on one is relying on luck.

	**The URL appears twice on purpose** — once as the button and once as text
	below it. A client that refuses to render the anchor, or a reader forwarding
	this to the machine they actually work on, still has something to copy. It is
	the one thing this message exists to deliver, and unlike an invitation it is
	also the only proof that the person reading it is the person who asked.
#}
<p style="margin: 0 0 16px 0;">{{ 'signup.mail.greeting'|trans({'%company%': company}) }}</p>

<p style="margin: 0 0 24px 0;">{{ 'signup.mail.body'|trans({'%slug%': slug}) }}</p>

<p style="margin: 0 0 24px 0;">
	<a href="{{ url }}" style="display: inline-block; padding: 12px 24px; background-color: #0d6efd; color: #ffffff; text-decoration: none; border-radius: 6px; font-weight: 600;">
		{{ 'signup.mail.button'|trans }}
	</a>
</p>

<p style="margin: 0 0 24px 0; word-break: break-all; font-size: 13px; color: #6c757d;">
	{{ url }}
</p>

<p style="margin: 0 0 16px 0;">{{ 'signup.mail.expiry'|trans({'%hours%': hours}) }}</p>

<p style="margin: 0; color: #6c757d; font-size: 13px;">{{ 'signup.mail.unexpected'|trans }}</p>
